      "scope": "Thirteen checkpoints over three windowed cases (window 17, 4 and 80) of a 150-row two-coefficient regression, including checkpoints on both sides of the product's internal rebuild boundary (rows 63 and 64) and a mean shift at row 90.",
      "independence_basis": "The generator rebuilds each trailing window from the declared row generator, solves the normal equations exactly in rational arithmetic and recomputes every summary directly; there is no incremental update, so the product's downdating is compared with a from-scratch fit. It imports no Antecedent code and agrees with every frozen value to 1e-12. The shared objects are the row generator formulas (restated in the generator and in the executing assertion) and the CUSUM definition, which is the maximum absolute running sum of the time-ordered residuals; that definition was identified by matching the frozen values.",
      "historical_exposure": "Original harness deleted (preserved in historical_oracle). The retained generator was written during this review from the fixture's declared inputs and the textbook definition; the product's assertion source was read for the row formulas and compared fields; no product rolling-update source was consulted. It reproduces every frozen value, so the frozen values were not products of a faulty product run. Who first derived the earlier values is unrecorded.",
      "redistribution": "Repository-authored MIT OR Apache-2.0 generator and deterministic inputs. Python standard library only, in an isolated locked script environment, outside the product and ordinary CI; no upstream source is stored.",
      "limitations": "Two coefficients (intercept and one slope), deterministic data, one seed-free series. The CUSUM is the unnormalised running residual sum, not a standardised changepoint statistic, and no detection power or false-alarm rate is claimed. The trigonometric row generator is evaluated in double precision in the product and in the generator, so agreement is to 1e-12, not exact. No calibration.",
